## SLNG Context Router

This agent's thinking goes through the SLNG Context Router. It caches the turns
this agent has already answered and serves them without calling the model, so a
repeated turn comes back in roughly a tenth of the time.

Every think request carries two identity headers:

| Header | Value |
|---|---|
| `X-Slng-Agent-Id` | the cache scope of whichever agent or task is speaking: the `agent_id` from the package, a colon, then that agent's or task's own name. It scopes the cache. |
| `X-Slng-Session-Id` | one uuid per call, created where the call begins. It groups this call's requests and scopes nothing. |

Each agent and each task sends a different scope, because each one has a different
prompt and the router's cache key does not include the prompt. Two agents sharing
one scope would answer each other's questions from cache. An agent's scope is its
own name; a task's is `task.` and then its name:

```
{{range .Slng.Scopes}}{{.}}
{{end}}```

You still write one `agent_id` and you still own its version suffix: bump it when a
prompt change should make old answers wrong, and every scope in the package retires
together.

A task runs on its owner's model service here, so entering one swaps the task's
scope in and every way back out swaps the owner's in again: finishing, moving to
the next step, handing the call to another agent, or failing.

Two consequences worth knowing. Two agents whose instructions happen to be
identical now get two scopes and stop sharing warmth. And a single-agent project
that upgrades to this compiler starts on a cold cache once, because its one scope
is spelled differently from the one it used before.

The model configuration travels inline in the request body, which means **this
agent sends the upstream provider's credentials to SLNG on every think request**.
Each one is read from the environment at startup and no file in this project holds
a value, but the credentials do leave for SLNG. Every name is in the startup check
above, so a missing one stops the agent at boot rather than mid-call.

The system prompt goes to the router with its `{{"{{"}}name{{"}}"}}` placeholders
intact, and this call's values travel beside it. That is what lets a personalised
prompt still repeat. A value over 4000 characters is truncated with a warning on
stderr and the call continues: an over-long variable never ends a call.

**The values are refreshed whenever the call writes one**, with a settings frame
queued right where a step's result lands on the call state. So a value this call
learns partway through is in the prompt from the next turn on, rather than being
the value the call started with. A name with no value yet is still sent, as an
empty string, because a placeholder the router was given no value for is a 422
that would end the call.

Which values may fill those placeholders is a rule, not a preference. A
placeholder is for a value this agent **says**: a caller's name, a company name,
an appointment detail read back aloud. Those are the values that make an answer
personal, and a personal answer is one the router would otherwise refuse to keep.

A value that changes **what the answer is** does not belong in one, however much
it varies per call. The reply language is the clearest case: two callers who chose
different languages would share one cached answer and one of them would hear the
wrong language. Write a steering value into the prompt text instead, and if two
variants must never receive each other's answers, give each its own `agent_id`,
which is the only thing that partitions the cache.

Nothing checks this. The compiler will not judge a value by its name, so a
steering value in a placeholder fails silently and only on a real call.
{{if .Slng.Vertex}}
The Vertex credential variable may hold the key JSON, that JSON base64 encoded, or
a path to the key file. The agent decides which at startup, in that order, and
fails at boot naming all three shapes if it is none of them. To produce the base64
form:

```sh
base64 < service-account.json | tr -d '\n'
```
{{end}}

### Params and the prompt directive

{{if .Slng.BodyParams}}Params on a router think binding travel **in the request body**, on both targets, and the router forwards keys it does not recognise to
the upstream. This project sends:

```
{{range .Slng.BodyParams}}{{.}}
{{end}}```

`world_part_override` never reaches the upstream: this compiler consumes it into
the router's regional base URL, and the line above says so. Everything else is
passthrough, and the compiler does not know what any of it means, so it cannot
tell you what the upstream will do with a value. If a value is wrong, the upstream says so and the
router relays the message intact, with its status code.

Constructor kwargs used to be where these went on LiveKit, and that is worth
knowing if you are reading an older project: a key the plugin's constructor had
never heard of was a `TypeError` at startup rather than a compile-time refusal.
The body takes any key.

{{else}}This project's router bindings send no forwardable params. Params on a router
think binding would travel in the request body, on both targets, and the router
forwards keys it does not recognise to the upstream.

{{end}}{{if .Slng.PromptSuffix}}An authored `prompt_suffix` is appended to **every
system prompt this binding sends**, separated by a blank line. This project
appends:

```
{{range .Slng.PromptSuffix}}{{.}}
{{end}}```

It reaches every agent's prompt, and every task's prompt on that binding's profile,
which on this target travels through its owning agent's model service. You can read
it in the `*_PROMPT` constants in the module beside this file, which is the point of
putting it there: a directive you cannot see is a directive you cannot check.

It is literal text and the compiler attaches no meaning to it. It exists because
some models take instructions no parameter carries.
{{end}}
### Reading the response

**Every think request logs one line saying where its answer came from**, at info
level, in every run:

```
slng router: scope=<agent_id>:intake source=cache layer=l2_exact request_id=req_...
slng router: scope=<agent_id>:intake source=llm model=<model> request_id=req_...
```

`source=cache` means no model ran. Repeat an exchange and watch it change: that
is the whole check, and it needs no dashboard. `layer` appears only on a hit,
`model` only on a live answer, and `request_id` is what to quote to SLNG support.
Count the lines if you want a hit rate; nothing aggregates them for you.

The line comes from a response hook on the client the router's LLM service builds,
because neither framework surfaces a response header. Those headers are the same
three below, which is what the hook reads:

| Header | Values | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `x-slng-response-source` | `llm`, `cache` | which path answered |
| `x-slng-cache-layer` | `l1_exact`, `l2_exact`, absent on the model path | which cache layer |
| `x-slng-model` | a model id, present only on the model path | which model answered |
| `x-slng-request-id` | always present, errors included | quote this to support |

**The first turn of every call takes the model path.** There is no preceding turn
to have cached. A conversation with no repeated turn in it will show no cache hits
at all, and that is correct.

**Not every repeated turn caches.** The router judges which turns are repeatable,
so a repeat served by the model is expected rather than a fault.

Tool turns always take the model path, both the request turn and the result turn.
Streamed responses carry no usage on either path, so token savings cannot be read
off the stream.

### When it fails

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| every tool turn returns 400, `Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported` | the reasoning models in the GPT-5 family reject function tools on chat completions without it | set `params.reasoning_effort: "none"` on the think binding. **Only that family**: on an OpenAI-compatible upstream the same param can itself be what the host answers with a 400 |
| a 4xx naming providers, models or a param you authored | the upstream refused a passthrough param, and the router relayed its message | read the message: it comes from the upstream and names what it accepts. The compiler does not validate a passthrough param, so this is where a wrong one surfaces |
| every think request returns 400, `cannot be resolved: no org config found` | the request reached the router without its inline configuration | this agent always sends it, so the body was stripped or malformed in transit |
| 400 starting `invalid slng_config:` | a malformed inline configuration | the message names the failing field; credentials are never echoed back |
| 400 `inline slng_config is not enabled` | inline configuration is off for the organisation | ask your SLNG contact to enable it |
| every think request returns 401 | the SLNG key is wrong or rotated | replace `SLNG_API_KEY` |
| an upstream 401 or quota error inside a 400 body | the upstream credential is wrong or out of credit | replace the value behind that upstream's variable |
| the agent refuses to start naming an environment variable | a declared credential has no value | set it. The startup check exists so this does not happen mid-call |
| the agent refuses to start naming three accepted shapes | a Vertex credential is not JSON, not base64 of JSON, and not a readable file path | fix the value |
| 422 `missing_template_variables` | a literal `{{"{{"}}` in the prompt that is not a placeholder | remove or rephrase it: the router reads any `{{"{{"}}` as a placeholder |
| answers stay slow across deploys | the agent id changed between them | keep one stable id and bump it deliberately |
| every turn says `source=llm` and the answers read back a number or a name | the value is rendered into the prompt, or the agent reformats it before saying it, so the stored copy holds the real thing and is refused | put the value in a `{{"{{"}}placeholder{{"}}"}}`, and give it the exact shape the agent speaks: the router substitutes back only where the answer holds the value character for character |

{{end}}## Deploy to Pipecat Cloud

Authenticate once first with `pipecat cloud auth login`. After that, one command
does the first deploy and every later one: `pipecat cloud deploy` builds the image
in the cloud from this project's `Dockerfile` and deploys it. That is why the
manifest names no image; naming one switches cloud builds off.
{{if .SecretSet}}
Create the secret set first, because the manifest already references it and a
deploy cannot start without it:

```sh
cp .env.example .env            # then fill in the values
pipecat cloud secrets set {{.SecretSet}} --file .env{{if .DeploymentRegion}} --region {{.DeploymentRegion}}{{end}}
pipecat cloud deploy
pipecat cloud agent status {{.AgentName}}
```

Run the same `secrets set` command again to change a value. If that set name is
already taken (secret-set names are globally unique across the platform), create
yours under another name and pass `--secrets <other-name>` on the deploy command,
which overrides the manifest.
{{- if .DailyCarrier}}

The set only needs the agent-side names listed under **Required environment**.
The helper-only names are read where you run the helper and the deployed agent
never reads them, so delete those lines from the copy you upload. Keep the
shared `{{.DailyCarrier.AuthTokenEnv}}` in the platform secret set: the bot uses
it to forward the existing carrier call.
{{- end}}
{{- else}}
This package declares no secrets, so there is no secret set to create:

```sh
pipecat cloud deploy
pipecat cloud agent status {{.AgentName}}
```
{{- end}}

Deploying the same agent name again updates the existing agent rather than making
a second one. Sessions already running finish on the old image, and a deploy that
fails leaves the previous ready version serving traffic. `status` reporting
`ready` is the deploy actually being usable.

### This agent's name

**{{.AgentName}}** is `name:` in your `agent.yaml` joined to the `{{.Target}}`
target it was compiled for, and it is `agent_name` in `pcc-deploy.toml`. Agent
names are unique across your organisation and the deploy above updates whichever
agent matches, so this name says which package this is rather than which
platform it runs on.{{if .SecretSet}} The secret set follows the same name,
because a secret set is per-organisation in the same way.{{end}}

Renaming it in the source package does not move this deployment. The next deploy
creates a second agent and the old one keeps running and keeps billing, so
delete it with `pipecat cloud agent delete {{.AgentName}}`{{if .SecretSet}} and
create the secret set again under the new name{{end}}.

{{if .WarmInstances}}The manifest holds **{{.WarmInstances}}** instance{{if gt .WarmInstances 1}}s{{end}} ready, from `warm_instances` on
this target in `targets.yaml`. That is a standing cost and it is what removes the
cold start. Change the number in the source package and recompile: this file is
rewritten on every compile, so the declaration belongs there and reaches every
later deploy on its own.{{else}}No warm instance is kept: the manifest declares no replica count, because the
source package declares none. So the first call after every idle period waits for
a container to start. Declare `warm_instances: 1` on this target in `targets.yaml`
and recompile to keep one ready, which is a standing cost and removes that wait.
`--min-agents <n>` on the deploy command does the same for one deploy only.{{end}}

### Reading this agent's log

```sh
pipecat cloud agent logs {{.AgentName}} -n 250
pipecat cloud agent logs {{.AgentName}} -s <session-id>
```

**Do not pass `-l DEBUG`.** It filters to *only* that level rather than setting a
minimum, so it hides the `ERROR` line that says why a session died. Run with no
`-l` at all.

The CLI wraps output at 80 columns, which corrupts its JSON output. For machine
reading, widen it first:

```sh
COLUMNS=100000 pipecat cloud --output json agent logs {{.AgentName}} -n 2000 -s <session-id>
```

`pipecat cloud agent sessions {{.AgentName}}` lists the sessions themselves, which is
how you tell a session that never started from one that failed inside. A session
marked `Complete` with a blank `Bot Start Seconds` never reached `bot()` at all.

### Region
{{if .DeploymentRegion}}
This agent deploys to `{{.DeploymentRegion}}`, from the manifest.{{if .SecretSet}} Its secret set
carries the same region, because a set is region-scoped and an agent can only
read one in its own region.{{end}}

A second region is a second agent, because agent names are globally unique
across regions:

```sh
{{if .SecretSet}}pipecat cloud secrets set {{.AgentName}}-<region>-secrets --file .env --region <region>
pipecat cloud deploy {{.AgentName}}-<region> --region <region> --secrets {{.AgentName}}-<region>-secrets
{{else}}pipecat cloud deploy {{.AgentName}}-<region> --region <region>
{{end}}```

`pipecat cloud regions list` prints the regions available to you.
{{- else}}
No region is declared, so the agent goes to your organisation's default region
(`us-west` unless you changed it).{{if .SecretSet}} A secret set defaults to
`us-west` regardless, so if you changed that organisation default, pass an
explicit `--region` to both commands: a set in the wrong region cannot be read
by the agent.{{end}} Set `deployment_region` on this target in `targets.yaml` to
choose one, and `pipecat cloud regions list` to see what is available.
{{- end}}
{{if .DailyCarrier}}
## Telephony setup

Setting this up is {{if or .DailyCarrier.HasOutbound .HasColdTransfer}}{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}four{{else}}three{{end}}{{else}}two{{end}} actions in your carrier's account and
{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}two commands{{else}}nothing{{end}} to run here. The carrier part is yours to do, because nobody but
you can sign in to your carrier account, so every step is written out.

### Who does what

Three parties are involved and it helps to know which is which before you start.

- **Your carrier**, named in the first part below, owns the phone number. It is
  where a caller's call arrives, and it is where you tell that number where to
  send the call.
- **Daily** is the audio bridge. Your carrier hands the call to Daily over SIP,
  and this agent joins the same Daily room the caller is in.
- **Pipecat Cloud** runs this agent. It is where you deployed it in the section
  above.

Nothing in this project touches your carrier's settings. The carrier actions below
are dictated for you to do.
{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}
### Why there is a helper

`telephony_helper.py`, in this directory, is the one piece that runs outside all
three. You run it, and here is why it has to exist.

Daily creates a new room for each call, and each room has its own SIP address. So
there is no single fixed address your carrier could be pointed at once and left
alone. Something has to be there the moment a call arrives, ask the platform to
start this agent on a fresh room with a SIP address on it, and keep the caller
hearing something while the agent boots. The agent hands the call over itself,
once Daily says that address is live. That is the whole job. The helper does not
make the room, does not answer the caller's questions, does not touch your
carrier's configuration, and keeps nothing between calls.

It has to be reachable whenever you want calls to land. Hosting it for real
(a public address, TLS) is yours to arrange; the tunnel below is for testing.
{{end}}
{{/* Action 2 is the incoming-call webhook, so it only exists when this package
receives calls. The two dial-out actions renumber behind it rather than leaving a
gap, because a numbered list that skips a number reads as a missing step. */}}
{{- $trunk := "3"}}{{$acl := "4"}}{{if not .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}{{$trunk = "2"}}{{$acl = "3"}}{{end}}
{{- if eq .DailyCarrier.Carrier "twilio"}}
### At your carrier (Twilio)

Do these in the [Twilio Console](https://console.twilio.com), in this order.

1. **A voice-capable number.** Phone Numbers, Manage, Buy a number, with the
   Voice capability. Skip this if you already own the number you want. If you set
   up the LiveKit target from this repository, the same account and the same
   number serve this one, and you already own everything in action {{$trunk}} as well.
{{- if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}
2. **Point the number at the helper.** Phone Numbers, Manage, Active Numbers,
   your number, Configure. Set "A call comes in" to a Webhook, method POST, at
   `$UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL/call`. `UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL` is the exact HTTPS base URL
   from the tunnel or production host (an optional path is allowed), with no
   trailing slash. Twilio signs that URL; a different host, path, or scheme is
   rejected before any agent session starts.
{{- end}}
{{- if or .DailyCarrier.HasOutbound .HasColdTransfer}}
{{$trunk}}. **A trunk termination address, because this agent places calls.** Elastic SIP
   Trunking, Manage, Trunks: create a trunk or reuse the one you have. On its
   Termination tab, set the Termination SIP URI to a domain ending in
   `pstn.twilio.com`. That whole domain is the value of
   `{{.DailyCarrier.SIPAddressEnv}}`. **Pick a prefix nobody could guess**, because
   action {{$acl}} authenticates callers by address rather than by password.
{{$acl}}. **Allow Daily's addresses to use that trunk.** Elastic SIP Trunking, Manage,
   Access Control Lists: create an IP access control list holding the `sip.hosts`
   entries from <https://ip-info.daily.co/ips/ip-info.json>, then attach it to the
   trunk's Termination under Authentication. There is no credential list to set
   up here and no username or password to store: Daily's outbound SIP carries no
   credentials on any documented surface, so the address list is the whole of it.
   That list changes; Daily publishes changes in the same file three days ahead.
   If termination starts rejecting calls, re-read it.
{{- end}}
{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}
Two console paths worth knowing, because moving a number between targets uses
both. A number joins or leaves a trunk on the trunk's **Numbers** tab (Elastic SIP
Trunking, Manage, Trunks, your trunk). Its webhook lives on **Phone Numbers,
Manage, Active Numbers**, your number, Configure. To read which of the two a
number currently uses, without clicking:

```sh
twilio api core incoming-phone-numbers list \
  --properties phoneNumber,trunkSid,voiceUrl
```

A `trunkSid` means the call goes to that trunk. An empty `trunkSid` with a
`voiceUrl` means it goes to that URL. See **One number serves one target at a
time** below for why that matters.
{{end}}
{{else}}
### At your carrier

We do not have dictated console paths for `{{.DailyCarrier.Carrier}}`, so here are
the obligations in your carrier's own terms. Its own guide is the place to look
for the exact screens; the route's documentation is
<https://docs.pipecat.ai/pipecat/telephony/daily-sip>.

1. **A voice-capable number**, one you own.
{{- if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}
2. **Point that number's incoming-call handling at the helper**, as an HTTP POST
   webhook to `https://<your helper host>/call`.
{{- end}}
{{- if or .DailyCarrier.HasOutbound .HasColdTransfer}}
{{$trunk}}. **A SIP trunk with a termination address** this agent can send calls to. That
   address is the value of `{{.DailyCarrier.SIPAddressEnv}}`. Choose a
   non-guessable prefix, because of action {{$acl}}.
{{$acl}}. **Allow Daily's SIP addresses on that trunk**, from the `sip.hosts` entries in
   <https://ip-info.daily.co/ips/ip-info.json>. This route authenticates by
   address list, not by credentials. The list is dynamic and changes are published
   in the same file three days ahead.
{{- end}}
{{end}}
### On this side
{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}
Two commands, and they are only needed for **incoming** calls. Deploy the agent
first: a call that lands before `agent status` reports `ready` gets the helper's
failure line rather than a conversation.

The first gives the helper a public address:

```sh
cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:7860
```

Copy the printed HTTPS origin into `UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL` in `.env`, then start the
helper in a second terminal. It must know that exact origin because Twilio signs
the full `$UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL/call` URL:

```sh
uv run telephony_helper.py
```

Any tunnel works; `ngrok http 7860` is the same thing. If its URL changes, update
both `.env` and carrier action 2 before restarting the helper.

Check it is up with `curl https://<your helper host>/healthz`, which answers
`{"status": "ok"}` and nothing else. It exists so you can tell "helper down" from
"helper up, something else broken".
{{else}}
Nothing to run. This package places calls and never receives them, so there is no
carrier webhook to answer: see **Place an outbound call** below, which talks to the platform
directly.
{{end}}{{if .DailyCarrier.HasOutbound}}
### Place an outbound call

Outbound is started against the platform, not against the helper: the helper is
only there to answer incoming calls. One command, and it needs nothing the deploy
did not already need.

```sh
curl -X POST https://api.pipecat.daily.co/v1/public/{{.AgentName}}/start \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PIPECAT_CLOUD_API_KEY" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "{\"createDailyRoom\": true,
       \"dailyRoomProperties\": {\"enable_dialout\": true},
       \"body\": {\"direction\": \"outbound\",
                  \"dialout\": {\"sip_uri\": \"sip:+15551230000@$SIP_TRUNK_HOSTNAME\"}}}"
```

Change the number; the trunk address comes from your environment, so there is
nothing to look up. The call leaves through **your own trunk**, which is why the
destination is a SIP address rather than a bare number.

**What the recipient sees is governed at your carrier**, by the trunk and the
numbers your account owns. This project does not choose it and cannot: nothing on
the dial-out side accepts a caller identity for a SIP destination. If the wrong
number shows, that is a carrier-side setting.

You do not need to buy a number from Daily for this. The dial-out approval named
under **Account prerequisites** is required, and it covers dialling a SIP address
exactly as it covers dialling a phone number.
{{end}}{{if .HasColdTransfer}}
### What a transfer costs

A completed transfer dials the destination **through your own trunk**, so that
leg is yours and it bills at your carrier's rate. Daily also stays in the call
path afterwards and both legs keep billing until the call ends. The alternative
would take Daily out of the path, but it needs your carrier to honour SIP REFER
on a leg neither platform documents that way, so this project does not use it.

The account prerequisite is the same dial-out approval under **Account
prerequisites**; there is nothing extra to turn on for transfers.
{{end}}
### Every step, in order, from a source change to a phone call

The whole loop, so nothing has to be pieced together from the sections above. Run
it from the **source package**, not from this directory: this directory is
generated output and recompiling overwrites it.

**1. Recompile after any source change.** From the repository that holds the
source package:

```sh
unmute validate <source-dir>
unmute compile <source-dir>
```

`validate` exits 0 and names any account prerequisite; `compile` rewrites
`<source-dir>/build/{{.Target}}/`, which is this directory. Skipping this after a
source edit deploys the previous version, which is the single easiest way to spend
an afternoon.

**2. Move into the build directory.** Every command below runs here:

```sh
cd <source-dir>/build/{{.Target}}
```

**3. Fill the environment, once.** `.env` is not regenerated over, so this
survives recompiles:

```sh
cp .env.example .env      # then fill in the values, agent side and helper side
```
{{if .SecretSet}}
**4. Push the secrets.** The platform reads them from the set, not from `.env`:

```sh
pipecat cloud secrets set {{.SecretSet}} --file .env{{if .DeploymentRegion}} --region {{.DeploymentRegion}}{{end}}
```

Re-run this whenever a value changes. The set only needs the agent-side names;
see the note in **Deploy to Pipecat Cloud** above.

**5. Deploy, and wait for ready.**
{{else}}
**4. Deploy, and wait for ready.**
{{end}}
```sh
pipecat cloud deploy
pipecat cloud agent status {{.AgentName}}
```

Do not place a call until `status` reports `ready`. Deploying again updates the
same agent rather than making a second one.
{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}
**{{if .SecretSet}}6{{else}}5{{end}}. Expose and start the helper**, in two terminals, then point your number at
the same signed URL per carrier action 2. Only needed once per session; update
both `UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL` and the carrier webhook when the tunnel URL changes.

```sh
cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:7860
```

Copy the printed HTTPS origin into `UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL` in `.env`, then:

```sh
uv run telephony_helper.py
```

**{{if .SecretSet}}7{{else}}6{{end}}. Test an incoming call.** Ring your own number from a phone. Expected: you
hear the hold line within a couple of seconds, then the agent speaks. Two log
lines, in two different places: the helper's terminal says
`inbound call ... started session ...`, and the agent's log says
`carrier call ... forwarded into the room` exactly once, however many times the
room signals ready.
{{end}}{{if .DailyCarrier.HasOutbound}}
**{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}{{if .SecretSet}}8{{else}}7{{end}}{{else}}{{if .SecretSet}}6{{else}}5{{end}}{{end}}. Test an outgoing call.** See **Place an outbound call** below. Expected: the number you
named rings, and the agent speaks when you answer.
{{end}}{{if .HasColdTransfer}}
**{{if .DailyCarrier.HasOutbound}}{{if .DailyCarrier.HasInbound}}{{if .SecretSet}}9{{else}}8{{end}}{{else}}{{if .SecretSet}}7{{else}}6{{end}}{{end}}{{else}}{{if .SecretSet}}8{{else}}7{{end}}{{end}}. Test a transfer, both ways.** On a call, ask for the person the agent can
hand you to. Expected: you hear the handoff line, the destination rings, and the
agent is gone once it answers. Then run it again with the destination **declining
or not answering**: expected is that you are still connected and the agent says so.
That second run is the one worth doing, because it is the path a caller actually
meets on a bad day.
{{end}}
Logs for any of it: `pipecat cloud agent logs {{.AgentName}}` for the agent, and the
helper's own terminal for everything before the call reached it.

### One number serves one target at a time

A number's Voice configuration is **either** a webhook **or** a SIP trunk, never
both, so there is never a question of which one answers: whichever the number
points at is the one that gets the call. Both agents can stay deployed the whole
time, and taking one down is not how you switch between them.

Moving a number is two changes, and the order matters.
**Take it off the trunk first, then set the webhook.**
Setting the webhook while the number is still on a trunk does not move it, and
that failure is silent: the trunk keeps taking the calls and this project's log
stays empty. Moving it back is the reverse, so clear the webhook and
**put it back on the trunk.**

Read where a number points before wondering which agent answered. It is a setting,
not a mystery, and the carrier part above has the exact command for reading it. If
two of your agents share a greeting and a voice they are indistinguishable by ear,
so read the configuration or the logs rather than listening.

### When something does not work

What the caller hears, and what it means:

| What happens | Where to look |
|---|---|
| Carrier error tone, or a recorded announcement | The webhook URL is wrong, or the helper is not reachable from the internet. Check `/healthz` through the tunnel URL, not through localhost. |
| Twilio reports HTTP 403 | The webhook URL and `UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL` differ, or the Twilio auth token is wrong. The helper validates the signature over every form field before it starts an agent. |
| A working agent answers, but not this one | The number is still on a SIP trunk, so the trunk took the call and this project never saw it. The helper's log is empty, which is the tell. See **One number serves one target at a time**. If two of your agents share a greeting and a voice, they are indistinguishable by ear, so read the log rather than listening. |
| The caller hears the hold line and nothing more | The agent never joined, or the forward failed. The helper's log names which: an agent that would not start logs the platform's own error text, and a forward that failed logs `forwarding carrier call ... failed`. |
| The caller hears the failure line straight away | The agent would not start, and the helper's log carries the platform's own words for why. `PCC-1001` means the deployment is not in a ready state, so check `agent status` before anything else. A missing platform secret and a name that was never deployed both land here too. |
{{- if .DailyCarrier.HasOutbound}}
| An outbound call never rings | The allow-list first (carrier action 4), then the termination address. Both fail the same way from the caller's side, and the bot's `dial-out stopped` log line carries the carrier's reason. |
{{- end}}

The helper never starts half-configured: a missing value stops it at startup with
the names printed, one per line. If it started, its own environment is complete.
{{end}}{{if .CloudWebsocket}}
## Telephony setup

**Nothing runs on your side and nothing is hosted by you.** Your carrier streams
the call's audio straight to Pipecat Cloud, which starts this agent.

{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasInbound}}The whole setup is **four steps**: three in your carrier's console, plus one
lookup command you can run anywhere.{{else}}The whole setup is **two steps**: one in your carrier's console, plus one lookup
command you can run anywhere.{{end}} In production there is nothing of yours to
run, host, or keep awake.

### Who does what

Two parties, and neither of them is you.

- **Twilio** owns the phone number and holds the small piece of call markup that
  sends a call's audio to the platform.
- **Pipecat Cloud** runs this agent and receives that audio directly.

The carrier part is yours to do, because nobody but you can sign in to your
carrier account, so every step is written out.

### At your carrier (Twilio)

Do these in the [Twilio Console](https://console.twilio.com), in this order.

1. **A voice-capable number you own in this Twilio account.** Phone Numbers,
   Manage, Buy a number, with the Voice capability. Skip this if you already own
   the number you want. If another target in this repository already uses a
   Twilio number, the same account and the same number serve this one.
{{if or .CloudWebsocket.HasOutbound .HasColdTransfer}}
   This same number is also what the person you call sees, and there is nothing
   else to enable on it: voice capability is the whole requirement. See
   **What the recipient sees** below for the two account-level things that can
   still refuse a call.
{{end}}{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasInbound}}
   Take the number off any SIP trunk first. A number attached to a trunk ignores
   its voice configuration completely, and it does so silently, so the markup you
   create in step 3 would never be consulted. See **One number serves one target
   at a time** below.
{{end}}
2. **Your organization name**, the one value the compiler cannot know:

   ```sh
   pipecat cloud organizations list
   ```

   **You want the machine slug, not your display name.** The output has more than
   one column and their headings differ between CLI versions, so go by the shape
   of the value rather than by the heading:

   ```text
   Organization Name   Organization ID           Active
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   your-display-name   three-random-words-12345  active
                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this one
   ```

   The value you want looks like `three-random-words-12345`: lowercase words
   joined by hyphens, ending in a number, never a space in it. Depending on your
   CLI version that column is headed **Organization ID** or just **Name**. It is
   never the human-readable name you chose, and never carries the `(active)`
   marker.

   Getting this wrong is the most common way this route fails, and it fails in the
   least helpful way: the caller hears the spoken line, then silence, and **your
   agent's log stays completely empty**, because a connection the platform refuses
   never reaches your agent at all.
{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasInbound}}
3. **Create a TwiML Bin** holding exactly this, with your organization name in
   place of `YOUR_ORGANIZATION`. Console path: TwiML, TwiML Bins, then the plus
   button. Name it anything; the content is what matters.

   ```xml
   <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
   <Response>
     <Say>Connecting you now.</Say>
     <Connect>
       <Stream url="{{.CloudWebsocket.StreamURL}}">
         <Parameter name="_pipecatCloudServiceHost" value="{{.AgentName}}.YOUR_ORGANIZATION"/>
       </Stream>
     </Connect>
   </Response>
   ```

   Four things in there are deliberate:

   - **The spoken line comes first.** Starting this agent takes a few seconds
     from cold, and a caller who hears nothing hangs up. Delete the `<Say>` line
     once you keep a warm instance ({{if .WarmInstances}}this deployment holds {{.WarmInstances}} ready{{else}}`warm_instances: 1` on this target{{end}}).
   - **The address is already the right one**, including the region: this file
     was generated for this deployment, so paste it as it is rather than copying
     one from a guide.
   - **The agent name is already filled in.** `YOUR_ORGANIZATION` is the only
     substitution you make.
   - **There is nothing else in it.** A `<Parameter>` reaches this agent only if
     the emitted code reads one by that name, and on an inbound call that is the
     service host alone. Adding the caller's number here is a common suggestion
     and it does nothing: no module in this package reads it.

4. **Point the number at that Bin.** Phone Numbers, Manage, Active Numbers,
   your number, Voice Configuration, "A call comes in", choose TwiML Bin, pick
   the one you just made, Save.

   Read the state back rather than testing by ear:

   ```sh
   twilio api core incoming-phone-numbers list \
     --properties phoneNumber,trunkSid,voiceUrl
   ```

   An empty `trunkSid` is what you want. A `trunkSid` with a value means the
   trunk still owns the call and your Bin is never reached.
{{end}}
### One region, three places

{{if .DeploymentRegion}}This agent's region is `{{.DeploymentRegion}}`, declared once in the source
package's `targets.yaml` as `deployment_region`.{{else}}This agent declares no region, so it goes to your organisation's default and the
address above is the default endpoint.{{end}} Three things are derived from that
one declaration, and the platform requires them to agree:

| What | Where it lands | Why it has to match |
|---|---|---|
| where this agent runs | `region` in `pcc-deploy.toml` | it is the deployment |
| where its secrets live | the `--region` on the `secrets set` command | an agent can only read a secret set from its own region |
| where your carrier streams to | the `wss://` address in the markup above | a regional stream endpoint routes **only** to agents deployed in that region |

Following this file keeps all three in step, because all three are rendered from
the same declaration. To move region, change that one line in `targets.yaml`,
recompile, and re-paste the address into your Bin. `pipecat cloud regions list`
prints what is available to you.

Two things bite when you move an agent that already exists:

- **Agent names are globally unique across regions**, so `{{.AgentName}}` cannot exist
  in two regions at once. Either delete the old deployment first, or deploy the new
  region under another name and change the agent half of the service host in your
  Bin to match it.
- **A secret set is region-scoped and its name is global.** Create one for the new
  region under a new name and pass `--secrets <that name>` on the deploy, or delete
  the old set first.

Where these steps come from, if you want the upstream reading:

- The working example this route is modelled on:
  <https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat-examples/tree/main/twilio-chatbot>
- The wire protocol between your carrier and the platform:
  <https://www.twilio.com/docs/voice/media-streams/websocket-messages>
- What a TwiML Bin is and how to create one:
  <https://help.twilio.com/articles/360043489573-Getting-started-with-TwiML-Bins>
- Regions, and the rule that the stream endpoint must match the deployment:
  <https://docs.pipecat.ai/pipecat-cloud/guides/regions>

### On this side

**Nothing, in production.** That is the whole section, and the absence is the
feature. Deploy per **Deploy to Pipecat Cloud** above, wait for `ready`, and the
number works.{{if .CloudWebsocket.CallEnv}} (This package places or redirects calls, so fill the
names under **Required environment** and push the secret set before deploying.){{end}}

### Hear it before the phone

`unmute dev <source-dir>` opens a browser session with this same agent: the same
prompt, the same tools, the same models, and no phone involved. Get the
conversation right there.

The phone leg itself starts where that stops, and it starts at a deployed agent.
Deploy, do the **Wire up Twilio** steps above, then call your number. A carrier
reaches you over the public internet, so nothing running on your laptop can
stand in for that.

{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasOutbound}}
### Place an outbound call

One command against Twilio's API. The TwiML rides inline, so there is nothing to
create in the console and nothing of yours for the call to come back to:

```sh
curl -X POST "https://api.twilio.com/2010-04-01/Accounts/$TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID/Calls.json" \
  -u "$TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID:$TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN" \
  --data-urlencode "To=+15551230000" \
  --data-urlencode "From=${{.CloudWebsocket.FromNumberEnv}}" \
  --data-urlencode 'Twiml=<Response><Connect><Stream url="{{.CloudWebsocket.StreamURL}}"><Parameter name="_pipecatCloudServiceHost" value="{{.AgentName}}.'"$PIPECAT_CLOUD_ORGANIZATION"'"/><Parameter name="direction" value="outbound"/></Stream></Connect></Response>'
```

Change `To`; everything else is read from your environment. Expected: the phone
rings showing your own number, and this agent speaks when it is answered. There
is no `<Say>` before the stream here on purpose, because a person is already
listening by then and the agent's greeting should be the first thing they hear.

Failures print in Twilio's own words. Nothing is swallowed and nothing reports
success it did not have.

### What the recipient sees

`From` is the caller identity. It must be **a voice-capable number you own in
this Twilio account, or a caller ID verified on it**, and when the destination is
a phone number the caller identity has to be a phone number too. It may be the
same number that receives calls, which is why one environment value serves both
directions here.

There is nothing to switch on for outbound on the number itself. Two things that
can still refuse a call live on the **account**:

- **The destination country's voice permissions.** Twilio keeps dialling
  permissions per country, and separately for low-risk, high-risk special
  service, and high-risk toll-fraud numbers. Console: Voice, Settings,
  Geographic Permissions.
- **A trial account can only call verified numbers.** The errors say so by
  number: `21219` and `14111`. Verify the recipient, or upgrade the account.
{{end}}{{if .HasColdTransfer}}
### What a transfer does, and what it cannot do

When the agent hands the caller to a person, one request replaces the live call's
markup at your carrier: a spoken line, then the destination dialled with ringback
audible, then a final hangup that ends the original call after that destination
leg finishes. The
destination is read from the environment at call time, so no phone number is
written into this project.

This is deliberately a cold transfer. If the person hangs up first, the original
call ends. If the destination declines or never answers, the original call also
ends after the dial timeout. No fresh agent starts with an empty conversation.

Pipecat `cloud-websocket` requires explicit `on_unavailable: hangup`; it cannot
reconnect the original media stream. Omitting the field resolves to
`return_to_caller`, so validation refuses it on this route. A successful carrier
REST update means the transfer has started, not that the destination answered;
the tool result is `transfer_started`.

**Warm transfer is not supported on any Pipecat target in Unmute.** Use the
LiveKit SIP route when the agent must brief the person before connecting the
caller. See the telephony and transfer overviews in the Unmute documentation
site.
{{end}}
### Security, in one paragraph

This route's deploy manifest sets `websocket_auth = "none"`, because a static
piece of markup in a carrier console cannot fetch a token first. What limits who
can start a session, then, is knowing the `{{.AgentName}}.<your organization>`
string your markup carries. Treat that string like a capability: it is not a
secret in the cryptographic sense, but anyone holding it can open sessions you
pay for. This is the platform's documented model for carriers that connect
directly.

### Every step, in order, from a source change to a phone call

Run it from the **source package**, not from this directory: this directory is
generated output and recompiling overwrites it.

**1. Recompile after any source change.**

```sh
unmute validate <source-dir>
unmute compile <source-dir>
```

**2. Move into the build directory.**

```sh
cd <source-dir>/build/{{.Target}}
```
{{if .SecretSet}}
**3. Fill the environment and push it.** `.env` is not regenerated over, so this
survives recompiles:

```sh
cp .env.example .env      # then fill in the values
pipecat cloud secrets set {{.SecretSet}} --file .env{{if .DeploymentRegion}} --region {{.DeploymentRegion}}{{end}}
```

**4. Deploy, and wait for ready.**
{{else}}
**3. Deploy, and wait for ready.**
{{end}}
```sh
pipecat cloud deploy
pipecat cloud agent status {{.AgentName}}
```

Do not place or expect a call until `status` reports `ready`.
{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasInbound}}
**{{if .SecretSet}}5{{else}}4{{end}}. Do the carrier part once**, per **At your carrier** above. It only changes
again if you change region or agent name.

**{{if .SecretSet}}6{{else}}5{{end}}. Test an incoming call.** Ring your own number. Expected: the spoken line
within a couple of seconds, then this agent.
{{end}}{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasOutbound}}
**{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasInbound}}{{if .SecretSet}}7{{else}}6{{end}}{{else}}{{if .SecretSet}}5{{else}}4{{end}}{{end}}. Test an outgoing call**, per **Place an outbound call** above.
{{end}}{{if .HasColdTransfer}}
**{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasOutbound}}{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasInbound}}{{if .SecretSet}}8{{else}}7{{end}}{{else}}{{if .SecretSet}}6{{else}}5{{end}}{{end}}{{else}}{{if .SecretSet}}7{{else}}6{{end}}{{end}}. Test a transfer, both ways.** On a call, ask for the person this agent can
hand you to. Expected: the accepted update ends the agent's stream, then the
carrier speaks the line and rings the destination. Let the person **hang up
first**: expected is the original call ending, with no new agent greeting. Then
run it a third time with
the destination **declining or not answering**: expected is the original call
ending after the dial finishes. Those last two runs cover the ordinary and
failure paths.
{{end}}
One log, and only one: `pipecat cloud agent logs {{.AgentName}}`. There is no
second place to look on this route, because there is no second thing running.

### If the call feels slow

The platform writes the numbers into that log, so read them before changing
anything. Two lines answer almost every latency question:

```text
[pcc-observability] First bot speech | latency=2.297s
[pcc-observability] Latency breakdown | events: ["...STT: TTFB 17.651s", "...LLM: TTFB 2.215s", ...]
```

- **First bot speech** counts from inside the process only. If it reads about two
  seconds and your caller waited much longer, the difference was the container
  starting: see the cold-start row above{{if not .WarmInstances}} and declare `warm_instances: 1`{{end}}.
- **The breakdown** attributes each turn to a service by name. One service far
  above the others is that provider, not this project and not the platform: take
  it up with them, or bind a different provider for that role in the source
  package and recompile.
- **End-of-turn detection sits between the two.** The pipeline waits to be sure
  the caller has finished before it answers, which costs a second or two per turn
  on purpose. It is the difference between an agent that interrupts and one that
  feels polite.
{{if .CloudWebsocket.HasInbound}}
### One number serves one target at a time

A number's Voice configuration is **either** a webhook or a TwiML Bin or a SIP
trunk, never two at once, so there is never a question of which answers:
whichever the number points at is the one that gets the call. Both of your agents
can stay deployed the whole time, and taking one down is not how you switch
between them.

Moving a number off a trunk and onto a Bin is two changes, and the order matters:
**take it off the trunk first, then set the Bin.** Setting the Bin while the
number is still on a trunk does not move it, and that failure is silent. Read
where a number points with the command in step 4 rather than wondering which
agent answered. If two of your agents share a greeting and a voice they are
indistinguishable by ear.
{{end}}
### When something does not work

| What happens | Where to look |
|---|---|
| A working agent answers, but not this one | The number is still on a SIP trunk or an old webhook, so your markup was never consulted. Read the number's configuration with the command above. |
| The call connects, the spoken line plays, then silence and a drop | Either the service host in your markup is wrong or this agent is not `ready`. **The tell is that this agent's log is empty**: a connection the platform refuses never reaches it. Check `pipecat cloud agent status {{.AgentName}}` first. Then re-read the service host: the agent name is `{{.AgentName}}`, and the organization is the **slug** from `pipecat cloud organizations list`, the one shaped like `three-random-words-12345`, never your display name (carrier action 2). Twilio's own call log names the stream error outright, under Monitor, Logs, Calls. |
| The spoken line plays, a long silence, then the agent | A **cold start**, and it is the single biggest thing you can fix. With no warm instance the platform has to start a container after the call arrives: schedule it, import pipecat, load the voice-activity and end-of-turn models. That is the silence, and it happens on the first call after every idle period, not just after a deploy. {{if .WarmInstances}}This deployment already holds {{.WarmInstances}} instance{{if gt .WarmInstances 1}}s{{end}} ready, so if you are reading this row the pool is not the cause: check `pipecat cloud agent status {{.AgentName}}` for a deploy that has not gone ready.{{else}}Declare `warm_instances: 1` on this target in `targets.yaml` and recompile, and it goes away: the number lands in this manifest as `[scaling] min_agents`, so it survives every later deploy. `pipecat cloud deploy --min-agents 1` fixes one deploy only, and a `[scaling]` block hand-added to the manifest does not survive the next `unmute compile`, which rewrites the file.{{end}} Your agent's own log tells you which half you are looking at: `[pcc-observability] First bot speech | latency=...` measures only the part inside the process, so if that says two seconds and the caller waited twelve, the other ten were the container starting. |
| The spoken line plays and the agent never arrives at all | The same cold start, past the point where the session gives up. A session that expires before the container is listening leaves an **empty session**: `pipecat cloud agent sessions <name>` shows it as `Complete` with a blank `Bot Start Seconds`, and the agent log has no `pipecat.workers.runner` line for it, because `bot()` was never called. Measured 2026-08-26: a session created at T+0 ended at T+10 while the server bound at T+12. So a warm instance is not only a latency nicety here, it is what makes the call answerable.{{if not .WarmInstances}} This target declares no `warm_instances`, so that is the first thing to change.{{end}} {{if .Knowledge.Knowledge}}This project has a knowledge base, which makes it much easier to hit: the corpus is embedded at import, before the server binds, so read the "Baking the index" section above{{if not .WarmInstances}} and declare `warm_instances: 1`{{end}}.{{end}} |
| Silence or degraded audio, everything else correct | Your markup names a different region than this deployment. The address in this file is the right one; re-paste it. |
| Nothing in the agent log at all | The call never reached the platform. That is the first row, or the number is not the one you think. |
{{- if .CloudWebsocket.HasOutbound}}
| The outbound command errors | Read Twilio's own words in the response. The two account-level causes are under **What the recipient sees**: the destination country's permissions, and the trial-account restriction on unverified numbers. Neither is a property of your number. |
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasColdTransfer}}
| The transfer never rings the destination | The per-call check refuses a missing destination by name at session start, so if the session started, read the agent log for the update request's response. |
{{- end}}
