## Trace with Coval

Tracing is configured. Set `COVAL_API_KEY` in `.env`. Spans go to
`https://api.coval.dev/v1/traces` under the name `{{.EntryAgent}}-{{.AgentName}}`.

**Where the traces appear.** A call Coval placed itself attaches to that
simulation's run. **Every other call**, deployed or local, appears under
**Conversations** and in **Trace Search**, and never inside a run. Looking for a
real phone call inside a run and not finding it is the most common way to
conclude that tracing is broken when it is working.

**Local against deployed.** `unmute dev` labels its runs
`{{.EntryAgent}}-{{.AgentName}}-local`; the same build deployed uses
`{{.EntryAgent}}-{{.AgentName}}` exactly. The label is decided when the agent
starts, not when it is compiled, so one build serves both. It is on the trace's
`agent.name`, on the conversation's `metadata.agent`, and on the conversation's
tag, which is what Trace Search filters on.

Each trace also records how the caller arrived in `coval.call.origin`:
`phone` for a carrier call, `websocket` for a plain socket session, `browser`
for WebRTC including `unmute dev`.

Every trace belongs to one Coval simulation, and Coval puts that simulation ID
on the call itself. The bot looks for it in four places, in this order:

1. `X-Coval-Simulation-Id` on the WebSocket upgrade request. This is the route
   Coval uses for a WebSocket agent.
2. The SIP headers inside a Pipecat Cloud dial-in body
   (`dialin_settings.sip_headers`).
3. A custom parameter on the carrier stream, which is where a Twilio
   `<Stream><Parameter name="X-Coval-Simulation-Id" .../>` arrives.
4. `COVAL_SIMULATION_ID` in the environment, for a local run or a route none of
   the above covers.

Point your Coval agent at this service and Coval fills the ID in for you. In the
Coval agent config the placeholder is `{{"{{"}}simulation_output_id{{"}}"}}`.

Calls that no simulation placed are traced too. When such a call ends, the bot
submits what was said to Coval as a conversation and exports the same spans
against the conversation ID that comes back, so a local `unmute dev` run or a
real customer call lands in Trace Search. Coval needs the call to exist before
spans can attach to it, which is why this happens at the end. The transcript
comes off the `stt` and `tts` spans the trace already holds.

Until either ID is known the bot holds its spans in memory rather than sending
them. The hold is capped, and once the cap is reached the oldest spans are
dropped with a warning in the log. If `COVAL_API_KEY` is unset the bot logs a
warning and runs without tracing; an evaluation credential never takes down a
live call. A call where nobody said anything is not submitted either, because an
empty conversation is not worth an entry.

Pipecat already names its own spans the way Coval expects, so what you see is
Pipecat's own `conversation`, `turn`, `llm`, `stt` and `tts` spans, not a second
set. {{if .NeedsMCP}}With Coval tracing enabled, the same calls emit `llm_tool_call` spans carrying `function.name`, `tool_call_id`, and `function.arguments`.
{{else if .NeedsFunctionCalls}}Each tool the agent runs adds one `llm_tool_call` span carrying
`function.name`, `tool_call_id` and `function.arguments`.
{{end}}One extra `transport` span records how the trace was correlated, in
`coval.correlation.method`: which of the four routes supplied the simulation ID,
or `conversation_submit` with `coval.conversation_id` when the call was
registered as a conversation instead. Read that first if a trace lands somewhere
unexpected.

These spans carry the attributes Coval trace metrics read, so the useful
metrics need no extra setup: percentiles over `metrics.ttfb` on `llm`, `tts`
and `stt` spans show where the time goes, and averages over `gen_ai.usage.*`
on `llm` spans show what the calls cost. An LLM-judged metric created with
traces included can also read each round's prompt and tool activity, so a
judge can check what the agent said against what its tools returned.

Pipecat tracing owns the process OpenTelemetry provider and startup fails if another SDK provider is installed first.
One provider serves the whole process, because Pipecat installs one and refuses
a second. Everything about *which* call is being traced resets on every call, so
a warm container that has already handled ten calls still files the eleventh as
its own conversation.

Starting the worker or exporting a synthetic span proves connectivity only.
Complete at least one user turn before reviewing the trace.
{{if .SecretSet}}
### Getting traces from the deployed agent

1. Put `COVAL_API_KEY` in `.env`.
2. Push it with the rest of the secrets, before or after you deploy:

   ```bash
   pipecat cloud secrets set {{.SecretSet}} --file .env{{if .DeploymentRegion}} --region {{.DeploymentRegion}}{{end}}
   ```

3. Deploy, place one call, and hang up. The trace is filed when the call ends,
   so nothing appears until then. Open **Observability → Conversations** in
   Coval and filter on `{{.EntryAgent}}-{{.AgentName}}`; the call is normally
   there within a few seconds of hangup, and a minute is the outside case.
4. If nothing appears, read the agent log. It says on startup whether
   `COVAL_API_KEY` is present and where traces are going, and it writes one line
   per call saying which conversation the call was filed as, or why it was not.

A Coval simulation that dials this agent on a plain phone number cannot deliver
its simulation ID, because the phone network strips the headers it travels in.
That call still traces, as a conversation, detached from the run. Nothing is
lost; it is just not where a run-based reader would look.
{{end}}

Traces can contain caller speech, model input and output, and tool arguments and results.
Use only fake identities and fake customer data for release tests, and do not
send real customer data until your Coval project's access and retention rules
are approved.

{{else if .Tracing}}
## Trace with Langfuse

Tracing is configured. Set `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY`, `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY`, and
`LANGFUSE_BASE_URL` in `.env`; all three are required. The trace name is
`{{.EntryAgent}}-{{.AgentName}}`; one trace contains the full conversation. The
Pipecat runner session ID is also the Pipecat conversation ID and Langfuse
session ID.
Pipecat tracing owns the process OpenTelemetry provider and startup fails if another SDK provider is installed first.
{{if .NeedsMCP}}With Langfuse tracing enabled, Pipecat MCP calls emit finite `tool:<name>` spans with tool arguments and, when completed, the result.
{{end}}

Starting the worker or exporting a synthetic span proves connectivity only.
Complete at least one user turn before reviewing the trace. A traced request
contains `conversation` and `turn` spans plus `llm` and `tts` generation
observations.

Traces can contain caller speech, model input and output, and tool arguments and results.
Use only fake identities and fake customer data for release tests. Use a separate
Langfuse project for those tests, and do not send real customer data until its
access and retention rules are approved.

